Approaches: Brute Force, Better, and Optimal

1. BRUTE FORCE APPROACH

Idea:
Create a temporary array containing all non-zero elements, then copy them back into the original array and fill the remaining positions with zeroes.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(n)
class Solution {
public:
    void moveZeroes(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<int> temp;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            if (nums[i] != 0) {
                temp.push_back(nums[i]);
            }
        }

        int j = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < temp.size(); i++) {
            nums[j++] = temp[i];
        }

        while (j < nums.size()) {
            nums[j++] = 0;
        }
    }
};


2. BETTER APPROACH

Idea:
Move all non-zero elements to the beginning of the original array using a pointer. Then fill the remaining positions with zeroes. This avoids using an extra array.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)
class Solution {
public:
    void moveZeroes(vector<int>& nums) {
        int j = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            if (nums[i] != 0) {
                nums[j] = nums[i];
                j++;
            }
        }

        while (j < nums.size()) {
            nums[j] = 0;
            j++;
        }
    }
};


3. OPTIMAL APPROACH

Idea:
Use two pointers. The first pointer j tracks the position where the next non-zero element should be placed. The second pointer i traverses the array. Whenever a non-zero element is found, swap it with nums[j] and increment j.

This moves zeroes to the end while preserving the relative order of non-zero elements and minimizing unnecessary operations.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)
class Solution {
public:
    void moveZeroes(vector<int>& nums) {
        int j = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            if (nums[i] != 0) {
                if (i != j) {
                    swap(nums[i], nums[j]);
                }
                j++;
            }
        }
    }
};



